                Every combinatorics problem begins with &quot;Permutations with Repetition.&quot; This is the Master Tree. It assumes you have infinite resources and order always matters. Everything else is just deleting what you don&#39;t want.
              &lt;/div&gt;
              &lt;div&gt;
                &lt;strong className=&quot;text-slate-900 block mb-1&quot;&gt;2. Deleting Reuse ($!$)&lt;/strong&gt;
                When you switch to &quot;Finite&quot;, the tree looks for any path that repeats an item and ghosts it out. The factorial ($!$) in formulas like $\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}$ is mathematically simulating this exact deletion of repeating branches.
              &lt;/div&gt;
              &lt;div&gt;
                &lt;strong className=&quot;text-slate-900 block mb-1&quot;&gt;3. Deleting Scrambles ($C$)&lt;/strong&gt;
                When you switch to &quot;Order Neutral&quot;, the tree ghosts out any path that isn&#39;t sorted alphabetically/numerically. This forces exactly one representative path for every group, deleting the scrambled duplicates.
              &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/div&gt;
          &lt;/div&gt;

